interactive shell.sh. I will run
it and now it's going to prompt

00:15:22.019 --> 00:15:25.979
us for our first name, or last
name, and then it will just

00:15:26.009 --> 00:15:28.469
print out our first and last
name back. So

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:33.300
we could have the terminal
display our own name, or

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:36.690
anything else provided in a
positional argument as well. But

00:15:36.690 --> 00:15:40.260
first, what is a positional
argument? A positional argument

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:43.320
is exactly what it sounds like,
we place the argument at a

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:46.230
certain position behind the
command or script we want to

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:51.180
run. The positional argument can
be in position, 123, and so

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:55.530
forth. The positions are always
separated by a space. When

00:15:55.530 --> 00:15:58.170
working with positional
arguments, you expect arguments

00:15:58.170 --> 00:16:01.230
to be in a certain position that
is separated by a space and

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:04.980
counting from one position zero
is reserved for the shell

00:16:04.980 --> 00:16:05.610
itself.

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:09.210
Now let's have a look at one of
these positional arguments.

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:14.850
Create a new script by typing
vim pulse ru.sh. And enter the

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:18.210
insert mode again, we will have
the script taking our first and

00:16:18.210 --> 00:16:21.570
last name and position one and
two and have it take these words

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:25.800
as positional arguments. Let's
just write our shebang and write

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:27.450
our one line of code like so.

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:35.430
shebang, Bin bash, Echo, hello.
Dollar sign $1 sign two. Now

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:39.030
save the file and close Vim,
change our permissions. And

00:16:39.030 --> 00:16:42.510
let's run the script again with
the positional arguments dot

00:16:42.540 --> 00:16:47.610
forward slash POS argue that sh,
herbert lindemans, or whatever

00:16:47.610 --> 00:16:50.460
your name is here. And as you
can see, we get the same output

00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:52.350
here with only one line of code.

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:58.740
One of the most frequently used
features in Bash is piping, it

00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:02.550
makes it very easy to perform a
specific action on the output of

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:06.270
the command. Let's say the
output of a command is very long

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:09.900
and you wish to filter something
out. Piping is going to give you

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:14.160
that option. Now how does piping
work? Let's have a look at an

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:14.790
example.

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:19.410
Let's say we want to look at a
specific directory. But we just

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:23.040
want to filter out certain files
or subdirectories. We could for

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:27.960
example, use a command like
this. So we type in LS minus L

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:31.680
forward slash our directory. In
this case, we're using the bin

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:36.510
directory at forward slash user
forward slash bin and then space

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:41.190
and then we type the piping
symbol SPACE grep, which is

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:44.880
something that we use to filter
out specific words and then we

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:50.790
type in bash. And we'll see the
output is just the bash binaries

00:17:51.090 --> 00:17:54.510
that we can see here. Rather
than the entire bin directory.

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:58.050
In this command, we forward the
output of the ls command into

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:02.310
the next command with the pipe
symbol. This symbol represents a

00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:05.580
pipe and this is what we call
piping. We literally sent the

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:08.490
output from a command before the
pipe symbol to the command

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:12.480
behind the pipe symbol. grep is
just an example there are many

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:15.960
more commands that have very
powerful potential when used

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:16.920
with piping.

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:22.080
When sending output to a file,
we have to use different kinds

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:26.730
of symbols than the pipe symbol.
Instead, we use the greater than

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:30.510
and double greater than symbols.
These symbols will be used to

00:18:30.510 --> 00:18:34.050
send the output from a command
to a file. This can have many

00:18:34.050 --> 00:18:37.050
potential use cases, the first
thing that comes to mind is

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:40.050
logging something from your
script to a log file. And this

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:42.300
is something that you'll
definitely use in real life

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:45.900
scenarios. Let's see how we can
use these symbols. First, we

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:49.470
catch the output of a simple
echo command. Start by typing in

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:55.260
Echo hello world greater than
hello dot exe. This prints hello

00:18:55.260 --> 00:18:59.190
world into a file called hello
dot txt. Notice that we put the

00:18:59.190 --> 00:19:02.400
command before the greater than
symbol and the name of the text

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:05.550
file after the greater than
symbol. Now we can see if this

00:19:05.550 --> 00:19:09.540
work by typing in cat hello dot
txt and we should see the output

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:12.900
of the echo command in here. Now
let's try something different

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:18.090
here typing echo Good day to
you. Greater than hello dot txt.

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:22.020
And let's see what's the hello
dot txt file by typing in cat

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:27.630
hello dot txt. But what is this,
the file was overwritten. We

00:19:27.630 --> 00:19:30.900
don't have our hello world
anymore. This is because the

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:34.260
greater than symbol always
overwrites any existing file.

00:19:35.070 --> 00:19:38.430
What we need to have is a proper
way to append text rather than

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:42.900
overwrite it. This is where the
double greater than symbol comes

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:46.650
in. This will append any output
to our destination file rather

00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:50.070
than overwrite it. Let's try it
out. But first let's remove that

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:55.590
original file with RM space
hello dot txt. Then we use Echo

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:59.790
hello world double greater than
symbol hello dot txt

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.510
We check if the contents are
indeed correct with cat hello

00:20:03.510 --> 00:20:09.000
dot exe. And indeed, the text is
there. And once we verified

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:12.150
this, we sent another output to
the file using the double

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:17.280
greater than symbol and type in
Echo Good day to you double

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:22.260
greater than hello dot exe. And
we once again verify this output

00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:26.910
with cat hello dot exe. And we
should see both outputs here.

00:20:27.810 --> 00:20:30.570
Now imagine using this in
combination with timestamps to

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:33.930
see which command ran at which
time and also some error

00:20:33.930 --> 00:20:37.410
handling. We're starting to
collect the tools we need to

00:20:37.410 --> 00:20:41.430
write real script, we can also
feed input into a command by

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:45.300
reversing the greater than
symbol, we can use the lesser

00:20:45.300 --> 00:20:49.980
than double lesser than or even
triple lesser than symbol to get

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:53.910
input from a file, multiple
lines of text or a single string

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:59.100
of text. Let's first look at the
lesser than operator, which will

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:03.240
get input from a txt file. We
will use the word count command

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:05.970
as an example here, because it's
one of those commands that has a

00:21:05.970 --> 00:21:10.320
use case for these types of
operators. Let's type in WC

00:21:10.530 --> 00:21:15.570
minus w hello dot exe to get the
word count. But what happens

00:21:15.570 --> 00:21:19.500
here, we get a little more than
we asked for as an output. The

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:23.880
wc command, by default also
shows the filename, which is not

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:27.690
what we want. If we just want to
get the number of words, we need

00:21:27.690 --> 00:21:31.890
to feed the wc command and input
from the file with the lesser

00:21:31.890 --> 00:21:35.490
than operator rather than the
file itself as a positional

00:21:35.490 --> 00:21:41.760
argument. This can be done like
so WC minus w space lesser than

00:21:41.790 --> 00:21:45.960
space hello dot txt, and this
way, we will redirect the file

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:49.590
to the wc command. Rather than
passing it as a positional

00:21:49.590 --> 00:21:52.680
argument to the command, we will
now get to the second way of

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:56.430
feeding data to a command with
the double lesser than operator.

00:21:56.610 --> 00:21:59.730
This is a great way to supply
multiple lines of text to a

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:03.630
command. When we write the
double lesser than operator, we

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:06.780
will immediately follow it up
with a word that will open and

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:10.440
close the text we want to input.
Basically, we tell the command

00:22:10.440 --> 00:22:14.190
line Hey, wait for this word,
and then capture everything I

00:22:14.190 --> 00:22:18.210
wrote in between the first and
second occurrence. Often people

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:22.710
will write e o f, but you can
write whatever you want. Let's

00:22:22.710 --> 00:22:26.970
have a look at how this would
work. So we type in cat space,

00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:31.260
double lesser than symbol elf.
Now we see that the command line

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:35.070
waits for input. So we can keep
writing lines of text here. And

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:38.130
let's write some text creating a
new line with the enter key

00:22:38.190 --> 00:22:45.210
we'll type in I will enter write
some enter text here, enter. And

00:22:45.210 --> 00:22:48.360
now we close it with the first
word we supply it and we type

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:52.230
again e o f. And now we should
see the text that we wrote here

00:22:52.230 --> 00:22:56.160
between the two e o f
statements. Now last but not

00:22:56.160 --> 00:22:59.610
least, we can also supply single
strings of text to the command

00:22:59.610 --> 00:23:03.900
line. This will be done with you
guessed it the triple lesser

00:23:03.900 --> 00:23:07.860
than operator. Let's have a look
at another example of the wc

00:23:07.860 --> 00:23:12.420
command we use. By default, wc
will either read a file or a

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:16.020
command output, but not actual
strings supplied as positional

00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:20.100
arguments. We should feed the
command either an entire file or

00:23:20.100 --> 00:23:23.160
the output of a file with one of
the lesser than operator

00:23:23.160 --> 00:23:27.540
variations. Let's see how we can
provide a string to this wc

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:33.750
command. So we typing w c minus
w again, space, triple lesser

00:23:33.780 --> 00:23:37.650
than symbol, and then we open
with double quotes Hello, their

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:41.160
word count. And then we close
again with double quotes and we

00:23:41.160 --> 00:23:43.920
should see the word count
printed here in the terminal.

00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:47.670
This is how we can feed a string
into a command and take note

00:23:47.670 --> 00:23:50.670
that the string needs to be in
between double quotes otherwise

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:51.600
it will not work.

00:23:54.780 --> 00:23:57.870
In bash, we have a built in
command called test that will

00:23:57.870 --> 00:24:00.990
take in a couple of arguments
and show you if the expression

00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:04.770
is true or not. For example, we
can ask a terminal to show us

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:07.980
whether or not a string of text
is equal to another. We can

00:24:07.980 --> 00:24:11.400
write test followed by the
expression, but I prefer using

00:24:11.400 --> 00:24:15.360
square brackets. Let's see how
that's done. So we just write

00:24:15.720 --> 00:24:19.290
open square brackets space. And
this is important because you

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:24.540
need the space in between Hello
space, equal sign space again

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:27.840
Hello, followed by another space
and enclosing the square

00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:31.230
brackets. And this will show no
output but when we do echo

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:35.610
dollar sign question mark. We
should see output zero here

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:39.180
because the first string of text
is equal to the second and that

00:24:39.180 --> 00:24:43.200
will give us a return value of
$0 sign question mark returns

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:46.620
the value of the exit code or
the last executed command. We

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:49.320
will learn more about exit codes
later. For now it's enough to

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:52.920
know that exit code zero means
that the command was executed

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:56.250
without any issues. Another
example of this would be

00:24:56.250 --> 00:24:59.970
comparing numbers with each
other. For example, again

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:05.100
We open the square brackets
space one equals zero space, we

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:08.940
close the square brackets, and
then we do echo dollar sign

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:12.750
question mark. And this shows us
that the values are not the same

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:15.630
because we get a return code
one. But we could also use a

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:18.240
different operator here to make
sure that the values are

00:25:18.300 --> 00:25:23.040
actually numerical. We could use
the minus EQ operator for this.

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:28.110
And we do this with opening
square brackets, one minus EQ,

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:33.270
one closing square brackets,
Echo dollar sign question mark.

00:25:33.300 --> 00:25:37.050
This also shows us the same
return value exit code zero,

00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:40.050
which means that the values do
equate, though this will throw

00:25:40.050 --> 00:25:42.630
an error if you would use
alphabetical characters instead

00:25:42.630 --> 00:25:43.650
of numerical ones.

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:50.130
Now for some more powerful
stuff, we can actually use these

00:25:50.130 --> 00:25:53.790
test expressions and perform an
action on it. Let's have a look

00:25:53.790 --> 00:25:57.390
at this more closely with an
actual script. Let's create a

00:25:57.390 --> 00:26:00.780
login script which will not
really log us in but it will

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:04.770
show us what is possible with
these if Elif else statements.

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:09.180
Create a new script called if
Elif else.sh.

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:14.610
Type in the shebang.

00:26:17.340 --> 00:26:23.400
Do F open the square brackets,
dollar sign, open curly braces,

00:26:23.550 --> 00:26:27.360
one comma comma close curly
braces and I'll explain to you

00:26:27.360 --> 00:26:32.970
what this means in just a second
equals your username, close the

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:37.860
square brackets, semicolon,
then, and then we follow up what

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:42.810
we actually want to do here. So
we want to echo Oh, you're the

00:26:42.810 --> 00:26:44.340
boss here. Welcome.

00:26:45.750 --> 00:26:51.300
Then start a new line. lf open
square brackets again, dollar

00:26:51.300 --> 00:26:56.070
sign, curly braces, one comma
comma curly braces equals help.

00:26:56.640 --> 00:27:02.430
Close the square brackets,
semicolon, then echo, just enter

00:27:02.430 --> 00:27:07.140
your user name data. Else. Echo.
I don't know who you are, but

00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:11.640
you're not the boss of me. And
then close it with FY. Now let

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:15.000
me explain to you what we did
here. So this script will take

00:27:15.030 --> 00:27:18.900
in a positional argument, which
is a great way to test how the

00:27:18.930 --> 00:27:20.220
if statements will work.

00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:25.620
We start our if statements with
an F followed by the test

00:27:25.620 --> 00:27:28.650
expression. Take note of the
square brackets used in previous

00:27:28.650 --> 00:27:32.640
section, we test if the first
positional argument is equal to

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:35.610
the value we provide. Take note
of the double comma and the

00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:39.300
curly braces. This is called a
parameter expansion. And it will

00:27:39.330 --> 00:27:42.300
allow us to ignore upper and
lower cases when comparing the

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:46.620
two values. We then end the test
operator with a semicolon. And

00:27:46.620 --> 00:27:51.090
we'll follow it up by then. And
then beginning a new line. And

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:53.940
on this line, we type what we
actually want to do with set

00:27:53.970 --> 00:27:57.690
test expression. In this case,
we just print something out to

00:27:57.690 --> 00:28:01.140
the console telling the user the
script has recognized the user

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:05.610
name and that he is indeed the
boss of the system. We can also

00:28:05.610 --> 00:28:09.690
have a script check for multiple
test expressions. We can define

00:28:09.690 --> 00:28:13.980
this with an elif statement.
This stands for else if being

00:28:13.980 --> 00:28:17.340
that else if the previous
statement wasn't true test if

00:28:17.340 --> 00:28:21.120
this is true. Again, start a new
line. We defined a test

00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:24.840
expression that will test if the
positional argument is help. And

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:28.230
if it is, we display a message
that shows us instructions on

00:28:28.230 --> 00:28:31.890
how to use the script. Being an
easy script we want to state

00:28:31.890 --> 00:28:35.220
that it isn't so hard to use.
Maybe a little rude, but it's

00:28:35.220 --> 00:28:39.120
all in good fun. Then if none of
these statements are true,

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:43.050
finally we have the else
statement. This will perform an

00:28:43.050 --> 00:28:47.400
action if none of the if or Elif
statements are true. We can

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:50.310
display a message saying that
the user name was not detected

00:28:50.310 --> 00:28:53.220
and that the system will not be
bossed around by someone that

00:28:53.220 --> 00:28:54.840
does not have the correct
username.

00:28:56.100 --> 00:28:59.970
Finally, to close our if
statements we ended with FYI.

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:04.920
This closes the entire block of
if Elif else statements and is

00:29:04.920 --> 00:29:05.580
required.

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:13.470
If Elif else statements are
useful, but you won't use them

00:29:13.470 --> 00:29:17.460
very often if you want to check
for multiple values. For these

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:21.660
kinds of things. We use case
statements. First of all, what

00:29:21.660 --> 00:29:25.770
is a case statement? Well, a
case statement performs

00:29:25.770 --> 00:29:30.150
different actions depending on
which case is true. This means

00:29:30.150 --> 00:29:33.000
that we can define multiple
options, and if the option

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:36.720
matches the case, we will
perform an action bound to that

00:29:36.720 --> 00:29:40.680
option. Let's have a look at how
this works in a real script.

00:29:41.250 --> 00:29:44.580
Let's try to recreate our
previous exercise with a case

00:29:44.610 --> 00:29:49.020
statement instead of the if Elif
else statements, create a new

00:29:49.020 --> 00:29:54.330
file with vim login.sh and type
in the shebang. And then we

00:29:54.330 --> 00:29:58.560
start our case statement by
typing in case space. Dollar

00:29:58.560 --> 00:29:59.970
sign and then

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:04.680
ie variable expansion with the
curly brackets, one comma,

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:08.280
comma, space in and over here,
we're going to do something

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:11.460
special, we're going to define
multiple options. Now this is

00:30:11.460 --> 00:30:14.790
something that you could also do
with if statements. But I'm just

00:30:14.790 --> 00:30:17.790
introducing it here with the
case statements. Since I thought

00:30:17.820 --> 00:30:20.850
it would be interesting to see
how it works. So we type in our

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:24.600
username, space, and then the
pipe symbol. And over here, this

00:30:24.600 --> 00:30:28.560
is actually like a separator for
multiple options, rather than

00:30:28.590 --> 00:30:33.870
the pipe symbol that we saw
earlier, to pipe things through

00:30:33.870 --> 00:30:36.930
two different commands. In a
case statement, it serves a

00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:40.020
different function over here, it
serves as a separator for

00:30:40.020 --> 00:30:45.570
multiple options. So user name,
space, pipe, symbol, space, and

00:30:45.570 --> 00:30:49.530
then another user name, for
example, the administrator, and

00:30:49.530 --> 00:30:54.060
then we close the bracket. And
then we define what we want to

00:30:54.090 --> 00:30:58.860
say or do here. So you want to
say something like, Oh, you're

00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:02.460
the boss here, welcome, again,
like we did in the previous

