to do this globally. G stands
for globally and globally means

00:47:01.260 --> 00:47:03.960
that we want to do this across
the entire text file, which

00:47:03.960 --> 00:47:06.780
means that we want to change
every occurrence, we close it

00:47:06.780 --> 00:47:10.110
with another single quote and we
put the text file we want to

00:47:10.110 --> 00:47:13.440
change behind the entire
command. Let's say we want to

00:47:13.440 --> 00:47:16.740
keep the original in a backup
file. We can do this by adding

00:47:16.740 --> 00:47:20.070
the minus II flag to the command
like so. So we just do the same

00:47:20.070 --> 00:47:24.810
thing we just do set minus i dot
original and then we enter the

00:47:24.810 --> 00:47:27.690
exact same command we just
provided earlier, we can see

00:47:27.690 --> 00:47:30.810
here that we use the minus II
flag, but we don't put a space

00:47:30.810 --> 00:47:34.560
behind it. Instead, we write the
entire suffix behind the minus

00:47:34.590 --> 00:47:37.560
II flag. This will create a
backup file which contains the

00:47:37.590 --> 00:47:40.230
original content of the file we
just changed with the set

00:47:40.230 --> 00:47:44.250
command but the original file
will have the changes we desired

00:47:44.280 --> 00:47:48.090
just like with aw k this is a
basic introduction to set and

00:47:48.090 --> 00:47:51.180
there will be so much more to it
but as with all things in it,

00:47:51.210 --> 00:47:53.850
you need to discover this by
yourself by trying it out as

00:47:53.850 --> 00:47:56.070
much as you can. Well this
brings us to the end of the

00:47:56.070 --> 00:47:56.670
course

